s a cross-record maker=actor join, not a six-dim patterns11:enforcements8:deferreds4:rootb1s4:texts42:no maker reviews or acts on its own objects4:thenl1:d1:s6:refuses3:SOPs4:whenl0:s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids3:SOPs8:polaritys1:-s9:record_ids6:rec_19d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:04.987377+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:04.987404+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:04.987399+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi20;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects12:OWNER-TUNNELs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd9:s11:enforced_bys84:tunnel-processor (unbuilt — the tunnel path is founded, no consumer op exists yet)s11:enforcements8:deferreds4:rootb1s4:texts73:owner may send instruction information to SYSTEM through the owner tunnels4:thenl1:d1:s9:append_ops21:owner-tunnel-consumers4:whenl0:s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids12:OWNER-TUNNELs8:polaritys1:+s9:record_ids6:rec_20d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:04.989802+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:04.989829+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:04.989824+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi21;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects15:EXTERNAL-TUNNELs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd9:s11:enforced_bys228:tunnel-processor (the general multi-entity consumer is unbuilt — EP-49B founds the law and builds the basic identity-at-tunnel verification in border.py; the general enforcement beyond a single attestor is C6's, design/51 §9)s11:enforcements8:deferreds4:rootb1s4:texts211:an established external entity's tunnel binds sender, target, permitted class and proof method; identity is verified at the tunnel; a peer's own claim is admitted as INPUT, never as a decision the record authorss4:thenl1:d1:s9:append_ops24:external-tunnel-consumers4:whenl0:s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids15:EXTERNAL-TUNNELs8:polaritys1:+s9:record_ids6:rec_21d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:04.996745+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:04.996772+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:04.996766+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi22;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects21:CONST-RECORDING-TOTALs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd11:s11:enforced_bys59:gate-machinery (audit-floor branch + the dual-audit mirror)s11:enforcements4:lives15:protected_packsl2:s18:dual-audit-actionss21:law-lifecycle-actionss4:rootb1s4:texts81:no governed act may be made invisible, and no amendment may lower the audit floors4:thenl1:d1:s6:refuses21:CONST-RECORDING-TOTALs4:tiers14:constitutionals4:whenl0:s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids21:CONST-RECORDING-TOTALs8:polaritys1:-s9:record_ids6:rec_22d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.000001+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.000026+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.000021+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi23;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects24:CONST-AUTHORITY-ANCHOREDs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd10:s11:enforced_bys311:the gate anchor guard (EP-18): succession is a recorded ceremony (DESIGNATE/ACCEPT/REVOKE-SUCCESSION), the current successor a live fold (verified + human re-checked at read), and any HANDOVER refuses without a valid successor — root authority never orphans and never passes to a pure-agentic/unverified actors11:enforcements4:lives4:rootb1s4:texts160:root authority is never orphaned (the owner may not remove their own authority without a valid human successor) and is never transferred to a pure-agentic actors4:thenl1:d1:s6:refuses24:CONST-AUTHORITY-ANCHOREDs4:tiers14:constitutionals4:whenl0:s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids24:CONST-AUTHORITY-ANCHOREDs8:polaritys1:-s9:record_ids6:rec_23d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.003118+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.003143+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.003138+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi24;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects13:CONST-SECRETSs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd10:s11:enforced_bys380:the secrets vault (EP-19; src/kernel/vault.py): SEAL-SECRET seals the value write-once with NO read path and records the hash only, VERIFY-SECRET compares by hash and records MATCH/NO-MATCH; there is no read op for a secret value (closure, not a cipher), so no actor — the chain-end included — can read it. On-disk protection of the vault files is campaign 4 (signing/crypto).s11:enforcements4:lives4:rootb1s4:texts121:secret values are unreadable by every actor including the owner; the record may verify a secret was used, never reveal its4:thenl1:d1:s6:refuses13:CONST-SECRETSs4:tiers14:constitutionals4:whenl0:s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids13:CONST-SECRETSs8:polaritys1:-s9:record_ids6:rec_24d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.005958+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.005989+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.005981+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi25;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects18:CONST-SELF-PROTECTs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd10:s11:enforced_bys50:gate-machinery (constitution-guard branches a/b/e)s11:enforcements4:lives4:rootb1s4:texts95:the constitutional records themselves refuse amendment and retirement; the tier protects itselfs4:thenl1:d1:s6:refuses8:BOOT-INTs4:tiers14:constitutionals4:whenl0:s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids18:CONST-SELF-PROTECTs8:polaritys1:-s9:record_ids6:rec_25d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.008739+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.008767+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.008759+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi26;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects21:CONST-SYSTEM-FUNCTIONs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd10:s11:enforced_bys79:gate-machinery (the untouchables floor, branch g + conservation branches d/e/f)s11:enforcements4:lives4:rootb1s4:texts153:no rule may destroy the system's capacity to boot from its record or to process rules at system level; space-local governance of rule-writing is not thiss4:thenl1:d1:s6:refuses8:BOOT-INTs4:tiers14:constitutionals4:whenl0:s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids21:CONST-SYSTEM-FUNCTIONs8:polaritys1:-s9:record_ids6:rec_26d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.012218+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.012248+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.012241+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi27;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects20:CONST-ACTOR-CATEGORYs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd11:s11:enforced_bys350:the constitution guard (gate.py _constitution_guard, amendment and retirement); the category read at every act (views.class_band at the pairing and the load act); the refusal of an established actor with no category (item 4); the write path's rule_cited for every classing act; the freeze of the axis and familiar-names packs (C8-P0a-i's freeze rule)s11:enforcements4:lives13:system_actorsl2:s10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:SYSTEMs4:rootb1s4:texts607:Every actor the system knows carries a category, read from the record: human, ai, program, or system — the system carries its own, the actors named in this row's system_actors. An actor's category must be assigned by a recorded act citing a rule, and it is consulted whenever the actor acts. The six axes every actor is placed by — category, S/U tags, single or organisation, seen, ruled, hardware — and these mind-kind words change only by descent, a new world citing its parent; the familiar-names word list changes only at a gov-os version birth. This rule refuses its own amendment and retirement.s4:thenl1:d1:s6:refuses20:CONST-ACTOR-CATEGORYs4:tiers14:constitutionals4:whenl0:s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids20:CONST-ACTOR-CATEGORYs8:polaritys1:-s9:record_ids6:rec_27d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.015002+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.015024+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.015019+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi28;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects28:CLASS-VERIFICATION-PROCEDUREs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd2:s4:texts270:How an actor's category is established: a program's and an ai's from the image sealed at its installation, a human's by the signing law at login; this procedure may be retuned by its authority, or retired by the founding's own act, without touching CONST-ACTOR-CATEGORY.s7:rule_ids28:CLASS-VERIFICATION-PROCEDUREs9:record_ids6:rec_28d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.018616+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.018644+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.018638+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi29;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects10:PACK-SCOPEs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd9:s11:enforced_bys156:the gate authority step over a vocabulary write (reach = a covering grant on category_pack info); reads sight-filtered (views.visible_category_packs); EP-17s11:enforcements4:lives4:rootb1s4:texts106:no rewriting a vocabulary beyond the actor's space or power reach; no reading one beyond the actor's sights4:thenl1:d1:s6:refuses10:PACK-SCOPEs4:whenl0:s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids10:PACK-SCOPEs8:polaritys1:-s9:record_ids6:rec_29d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.021366+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.021391+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.021386+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi30;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects11:TICK-POLICYs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd2:s4:texts95:the timer-tick input class shall be admitted only as recorded sensor arrivals (clock-as-sensor)s7:rule_ids11:TICK-POLICYs9:record_ids6:rec_30d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.023529+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.023554+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.023548+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi31;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects11:WAIT-POLICYs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd2:s4:texts77:an actor may record a block awaiting a condition; a matching arrival wakes its7:rule_ids11:WAIT-POLICYs9:record_ids6:rec_31d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.026368+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.026394+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.026389+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi32;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects13:WATCHER-BRAKEs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd2:s4:texts153:a watched actor may freeze its watcher's ops pending owner resolution; the brake halts the watcher only, never the recording (the record never goes dark)s7:rule_ids13:WATCHER-BRAKEs9:record_ids6:rec_32d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.028481+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.028530+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.028524+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi33;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects14:COMPARISON-LAWs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd2:s4:texts220:verdicts from DIFFERENT frames are different information — never a conflict, both kept, no merger; only SAME-frame incompatibility is a contradiction, routed to the owner. Compare without merging (the federation seed).s7:rule_ids14:COMPARISON-LAWs9:record_ids6:rec_33d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.030531+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.030554+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.030549+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi34;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects8:DICT-LAWs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd2:s4:texts224:terms are reusable dictionary records (action/resource/actor); the current entry per term is latest-undisputed-wins (a fold, never a status); a dispute routes to the owner; a definition_ref must resolve at the moment of use.s7:rule_ids8:DICT-LAWs9:record_ids6:rec_34d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.032607+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.032633+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.032628+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi35;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects24:AUTHORITY-REGIME-DEFAULTs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd4:s10:policy_keys24:authority-regime-defaults4:texts284:the DEFAULT authority regime the gate applies to an op that declares none is `covers` (covers-or-refuse over the four-dimension grant fold); the four power-structure ops (grant/revoke/space/role) declare `attenuation-family`. The default is pack DATA (EP-18 R-A), not a code constant.s5:values6:coverss7:rule_ids24:AUTHORITY-REGIME-DEFAULTs9:record_ids6:rec_35d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.036170+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.036196+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.036191+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi36;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects20:CROSSING-CORRELATIONs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd2:s4:texts394:an answer from outside the S-plane binds to its hand-out by the STORE's own citation: a crossing record naming no hand-out, or naming one already answered or superseded by a re-judge, is refused. What a record claims about itself in its own content carries no binding — only the store's record of the hand-out does, because nothing binds a token to a hand-out except the store's record of it.s7:rule_ids20:CROSSING-CORRELATIONs9:record_ids6:rec_36d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.038387+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.038408+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.038403+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi37;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects14:CROSSING-STALEs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd2:s4:texts578:an external answer is accepted only against the world it was judged in: the hand-out records the canonical hash of its declared input-view, the return recomputes that view under the same pinned version, and an answer whose seal moved — or whose acceptance ran no seal check at all — is refused. Staleness is not an authority verdict and never substitutes for one: a chain revoked mid-crossing refuses on authority with the seal holding, and a moved read-set refuses on staleness with every act still lawful. The two verdicts carry different information and are never merged.s7:rule_ids14:CROSSING-STALEs9:record_ids6:rec_37d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.041569+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.041597+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.041591+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi38;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects21:CROSSING-STALE-POLICYs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd4:s10:policy_keys29:crossing-stale-policy-defaults4:texts445:what happens when an answer returns stale, unless the station names its own policy under `crossing-stale-policy:<STATION>`: `re-judge` mints a fresh crossing at the current head, parent-linked to the stale one, and `abort` leaves the stale refusal standing. Never a silent execute; never a silent drop — the refusal is recorded citing the staleness rule either way, and a case the machine may not retry surfaces on the re-ask view for a human.s5:values8:re-judges7:rule_ids21:CROSSING-STALE-POLICYs9:record_ids6:rec_38d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.044794+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.044818+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.044813+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi39;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects21:CROSSING-RETRY-BUDGETs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd4:s10:policy_keys29:crossing-retry-budget-defaults4:texts441:how many re-judges one crossing chain may spend before the stale refusal stands and the case surfaces for a human re-ask, unless the station names its own budget under `crossing-retry-budget:<STATION>`. A retry budget is recorded policy with a named owner (the root, until a grant says otherwise), never a constant in code; what a chain has already spent is counted from the parent links in the record, so there is no attempt counter to rot.s5:valuei2;s7:rule_ids21:CROSSING-RETRY-BUDGETs9:record_ids6:rec_39d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.046907+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.046928+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.046922+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi40;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects20:COMM-LAW-SHARE-GRANTs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys462:the operation's own `require_prior` row over the region, against MEM-GRANT's `region` field — AND NOTHING ELSE YET. The grantee side and the granter-holds side are unenforced and the rule's text says so at its own hand rather than leaving a reader to discover it: EP-30-K2 (a quantified kind) and EP-30-C4W (the wiring) carry them. `src/subsystems/comms.py`'s fold over `by_action("SHM-GRANT")` holds no stored holder field and is rebuilt from the record alones11:enforcements4:lives4:texts2067:a SHARE of a region moves from one entity to others by a recorded, rule-citing decision that NAMES THE REGION IT IS OVER, and the region is one A PRIOR RECORD FOUNDED rather than a string the caller supplies. THE GRANT INHERITS THE REGION'S ESTABLISHMENT AND NEVER MINTS IT: `MEM-GRANT` is the region's establishing act (`object_param: region`, its payload carrying `region` and `holder`), so a share over a region no MEM-GRANT founds is a grant of nothing, appended as though it were a grant of something — it is REFUSED, citing this rule. One identity derived twice is two answers waiting to disagree, and the disagreement would be invisible because both sides would be internally consistent. WHO SHARES WHAT NOW IS DERIVED, never stored: it is a fold of these decisions, so killing every derived structure and replaying the record alone returns the same answer. The granter is an ATTRIBUTION STAMPED TO THE ACTING ACTOR and is never read from the caller's supplied value. SEEDED with the operation that cites it, because no refusal path may cite a rule name the founding does not contain (the EP-25 ADDENDUM 4 containment line) — this operation has cited this rule since it shipped and the pack has never held it, so until now its every refusal would have named nothing. WHAT THIS RULE DOES NOT YET REACH, STATED IN THE LAW ITSELF SO NO READER INFERS COVER IT DOES NOT HAVE: it does not ask WHETHER THE PARTIES BEING GRANTED ACCESS ARE ESTABLISHED ENTITIES, and it does not ask WHETHER THE GRANTER HOLDS THE REGION. Neither omission is a choice. `grantees` is MANY-VALUED and the check vocabulary HAS NO QUANTIFIER, so a many-valued parameter cannot be asked anything at all — not every, not any, not none; the capability rides EP-30-K2 and the wiring rides EP-30-C4W. The granter question needs a record bound by one parameter and a field of it compared against another, which is EP-30-K1's `bound_field`, and wiring it onto this operation rides EP-30-C4W. A LAW THAT CLAIMED THE COVER IT DOES NOT HAVE WOULD BE WORSE THAN THE DANGLING CITATION IT REPLACES.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids20:COMM-LAW-SHARE-GRANTs9:record_ids6:rec_40d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.050670+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.050726+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.050712+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi41;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects11:FS-LAW-PERMs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys151:the gate authority step over the custody ops (covers-or-refuse), and the mount's own permission fold, which reads recorded law and never a stored tables11:enforcements4:lives4:texts509:custody of a file's CONTENT and of the permission that governs it: an open, a write, a truncate, a mode/ownership/timestamp change and an ordinary extended-attribute write are each a recorded, rule-citing decision, and content is content-addressed full-fidelity with the event recording the hash. Cited by the files custody ops since the campaign-1 cores; SEEDED as a record here at the crux (EP-25 ADDENDUM 4) so that no custody definition and no refusal path cites a rule name the founding does not contain.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids11:FS-LAW-PERMs9:record_ids6:rec_41d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.054280+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.054301+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.054296+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi42;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects16:FS-LAW-NAMESPACEs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd6:s11:enforced_bys135:the gate authority step over the namespace ops; the tree itself is a derived cache of these decisions and holds no authority of its owns11:enforcements4:lives4:texts777:custody of the NAMESPACE: create, mkdir, link, symlink, rename, unlink and rmdir are recorded, rule-citing decisions over names. Deletion is namespace removal — a name leaves the living tree while the blob's history persists in the ledger, so the POSIX contract stays exact for the living namespace and asOf stays native. THIS KEY SPACE HAS A ROOT, AND ONLY this law can say so: every other key here is bound by an act that a record carries, and the root is bound by NO act — nothing creates it, so a fold over acts can never contain it, and a hierarchy question over any top-level name would otherwise be refused for landing in a container that no record founds. The root is therefore declared, with what it names, and the engine holds no root and no separator of its own.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids16:FS-LAW-NAMESPACEs9:key_spaced2:s4:roots1:/s9:root_kinds3:dirs9:record_ids6:rec_42d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.056763+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.056785+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.056779+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi43;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects12:FS-LAW-MOUNTs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys67:the MOUNT / UNMOUNT op definitions and the gate's ordinary law paths11:enforcements4:lives4:texts161:mount rules are LAW: attaching, configuring, or detaching a filesystem must be a recorded, authorized amendment to the namespace (design/10 §6, the mount rows).s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids12:FS-LAW-MOUNTs9:record_ids6:rec_43d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.058785+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.058814+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.058806+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi44;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects22:FS-LAW-DIVERGENCE-HALTs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys131:the mount's continuous shadow-diff, which compares the incrementally derived state against the same fold recomputed from the records11:enforcements4:lives4:texts481:the assume's own safety catch (design/16 §4; the failure book's divergence law): when the state derived by replaying the record alone differs from the state the mount is serving, the assume HALTS — the mount goes read-only — and the divergence is recorded, citing this rule. Both failure directions are governed: a divergence that fails to halt is the defect this rule exists to catch, and a halt raised where the two agree would block a lawful assume and is equally a defect.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids22:FS-LAW-DIVERGENCE-HALTs9:record_ids6:rec_44d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.061071+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.061094+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.061088+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi45;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects11:FS-LAW-LOCKs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys205:the gate authority step over FILE-LOCK / FILE-UNLOCK (covers-or-refuse), and the mount's own lock-exclusion fold, which is derived from the recorded lock decisions and holds no stored lock table of its owns11:enforcements4:lives4:texts1016:custody of EXCLUSIVITY over a byte range (design/10 §6, the fcntl and flock rows: a lock grant is a DECISION — recorded, cites rule). A lock is granted by a recorded decision and is REFUSED, citing this rule, when another owner already holds an overlapping lock that cannot be shared — two write locks, or a write against a read. The caller sees the POSIX errno for the precondition (EAGAIN on a non-blocking conflict) and the record carries the rule, exactly as every other refusal at this port does. The release is recorded too: a lock dropped explicitly, or dropped because the holder closed a descriptor on the file (the POSIX rule that closing ANY descriptor for a file releases that process's locks on it), appends its own decision, so the span a caller held exclusivity for is reconstructible from the record alone. SEEDED here with the ops that cite it (EP-25 ADDENDUM 4, the owner's containment line), because the lock ops are new and no refusal path may cite a rule name the founding does not contain.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids11:FS-LAW-LOCKs9:record_ids6:rec_45d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.063250+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.063267+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.063264+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi46;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects19:FS-WRITE-COALESCINGs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s10:policy_keys22:files-write-coalescings4:texts841:WRITE-EVENT GRANULARITY, the named calibration design/10 §11.4 requires (many small writes -> one recorded event citing this policy, never a silent loss). PROPOSED DEFAULT, awaiting the owner's value (design/36 §11 item 5): writes to one open file description accumulate as a burst, and the burst is CLOSED — one covering DECISION appended, citing this policy and carrying the content hash of every byte — by whichever comes first: the descriptor closing, an fsync, `max_bytes` of accumulated content, or `max_events` accumulated writes. Every byte is content-addressed either way; what the policy changes is how many events carry them. FAIL-CLOSED: with this policy absent from the record no coalescing occurs and every write appends its own covering decision — the safe direction, since recording more is never the defect K3 names.s5:scopes10:space:roots5:valued4:s10:max_eventsi64;s4:modes12:per-fd-bursts7:closersl4:s5:closes5:fsyncs9:max_bytess10:max_eventss9:max_bytesi1048576;s7:rule_ids19:FS-WRITE-COALESCINGs9:record_ids6:rec_46d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.065323+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.065349+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.065342+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi47;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects19:FS-READ-AGGREGATIONs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s10:policy_keys22:files-read-aggregations4:texts513:READ-TRAFFIC granularity. Reads are STREAM for the audit aggregate and the caller's bytes are always exact (design/10 §11.5 — payload fidelity is independent of the classification). PROPOSED DEFAULT, the same owner calibration: read traffic is counted per inode and one STREAM-class aggregate is appended citing this policy when `max_reads` or `max_bytes` is reached, or when the mount unmounts. No read appends a record of its own, which is what keeps recording tracking governance rather than operation (K3).s5:scopes10:space:roots5:valued4:s4:modes9:per-inodes7:closersl3:s9:max_readss9:max_bytess7:unmounts9:max_bytesi67108864;s9:max_readsi4096;s7:rule_ids19:FS-READ-AGGREGATIONs9:record_ids6:rec_47d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.068350+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.068370+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.068367+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi48;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects23:FS-LAW-CUSTODY-TRANSFERs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys305:the gate authority step over FILE-CUSTODY-TRANSFER (covers-or-refuse), the operation's own require_prior rows over the two parties, the open handle and the bound device, and `bridge/custody.py`'s HandleCustody fold, which holds no stored holder field and is rebuilt from the record alone by `fold_custody`s11:enforcements4:lives4:texts1638:custody of an OPEN HANDLE moves between ENTITIES by a recorded, rule-citing decision that NAMES BOTH PARTIES (design/10 §8: fd passing is itself a recorded custody transfer, and the pidfd_getfd row calls moving a custody grant across processes a DECISION; design/39 §4: handing another program an open file is a custody transfer THROUGH the border, recorded). A HANDOVER IS AN ACT BETWEEN TWO PARTIES AND NEVER A PROPERTY OF THE FILE. The file is not a party to it, so an attribute on the file cannot carry it: an attribute value is any string a caller writes, while a party is a recorded establishment, and a transfer whose giver or receiver is a bare value names nobody — it is REFUSED, citing this rule. That refusal is what makes the entity vocabulary established rather than merely declared. THE DEVICE THE HANDLE STANDS ON IS INHERITED AND NEVER RE-DERIVED: the transfer names a device a prior binding founded under DEV-LAW-BIND, and the channel side computes no identity of its own — one identity derived twice is two answers waiting to disagree, and the disagreement would be invisible because both sides would be internally consistent. WHO HOLDS A HANDLE NOW IS DERIVED, never stored: it is a fold of these decisions, latest-wins, so killing every derived structure and replaying the record alone returns the same answer. A stored holder field is a frozen copy of the record and rots away from it silently, which is the one failure a derived answer cannot have. SEEDED here with the operation that cites it, because no refusal path may cite a rule name the founding does not contain (the EP-25 ADDENDUM 4 containment line).s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids23:FS-LAW-CUSTODY-TRANSFERs9:record_ids6:rec_48d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.071508+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.071538+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.071531+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi49;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects16:TWO-TIMES-ACCEPTs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd2:s4:texts1238:every judged or authorised act has two times, and they carry different halves. The DECIDING time is the moment the thing became authorised in the decider's hands, and that is where legitimacy is created. The RECORDING time is the moment the store wrote it, and that is where order is created; record_time remains the sole total-order anchor and nothing in this rule moves it. A decision binds from its deciding moment, not from its arrival: arrival order never adjudicates. The store's acceptance test is a legitimacy-transfer test — an arriving decision is evaluated against the law in force AS OF ITS DECIDING TIME, with its seal as proof of the world it was decided in, while its actor's AUTHORITY is evaluated LIVE at effect time. A chain revoked between the deciding moment and the arrival refuses at the door however sound the deciding time was, and that refusal carries authority information rather than staleness information; the two verdicts are never merged. A law-family record whose deciding time EQUALS an act's deciding time is not in force for that act — the comparison is strict, at the ordinary door and in the reconciliation sweep alike, because one rule applied twice cannot resolve the same pair of facts two ways.s7:rule_ids16:TWO-TIMES-ACCEPTs9:record_ids6:rec_49d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.074333+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.074360+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.074354+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi50;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects18:TWO-TIMES-OVERTURNs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd2:s4:texts1664:when law arrives whose deciding time precedes already-recorded acts, the store rewrites nothing. It computes which recorded acts the law reaches — acts decided strictly after the law's own deciding moment and recorded before the law arrived, whose admission the law flips — and appends OVERTURN decisions, each citing the reaching law (or, where the reach was transitive, the overturned ancestor whose exclusion reached it), the overturned act, and both deciding times. Views answer with the reconciled state from the overturn's own recording time forward; an asOf answer from before the overturn still shows the act's effect, because order is untouched and validity is what changed. Overturning is ordinary governed activity: recorded, rule-citing, attributable, replayable, and subject to every standing check including the protected-core floor and the conservation law. A REFUSAL is never converted into a done act: a late-arriving permissive law appends nothing, because an overturn cannot perform an act that never happened and a record claiming an effect that did not occur is the one forbidden lie — the case surfaces for re-asking and the actor re-submits at the current head. An act once overturned is never un-overturned. A sweep computes its rounds to a fixed point before it appends anything, and appends nothing at all if the post-state it computed would leave any chain without a root or move root; that sweep routes to the owner instead. A law-family arrival that would reach recorded acts is not accepted while a sweep is running — the running sweep settles the record first, so two identical inputs cannot give two results by interleaving.s7:rule_ids18:TWO-TIMES-OVERTURNs9:record_ids6:rec_50d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.076696+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.076718+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.076712+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi51;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects20:TWO-TIMES-SEAL-BOUNDs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd2:s4:texts668:a claimed deciding time cannot predate the store-state its own seal pins. Deciding timestamps are caller-asserted until they are cryptographically signed, and that cap is stated rather than hidden; what the seal closes is one direction of it. The seal is the recorded hash of exactly what the decider was shown, so the world it pins has a first moment, and a decision cannot be claimed at 9:00 over a world that only came into being at 9:10. An arrival whose claimed deciding time no store state at or before it could have produced is refused, cited. The other direction — a claimed time running later than the true one — stays open until record signing closes it.s7:rule_ids20:TWO-TIMES-SEAL-BOUNDs9:record_ids6:rec_51d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.084882+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.084901+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.084898+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi52;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects16:SESSION-DECLAREDs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd2:s4:texts721:a decider's live working context is recorded: a session-open naming the account and its read-set, and a session-close ending it. The registry is a derived view over those two records and holds nothing. The read-set is DECLARED rather than derived, and that is necessity rather than laziness — reads append nothing by design, so the store cannot compute what a session has looked at. Under-declaration costs a session deliveries it would have wanted; it never costs legitimacy, because the seal still faces every decision at acceptance. A read-set is declared as view references and resolved at use, never stored resolved, because a resolved copy is a frozen derivative of a definition that can be amended underneath it.s7:rule_ids16:SESSION-DECLAREDs9:record_ids6:rec_52d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.086637+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.086652+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.086649+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi53;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects12:LANE-BUDGETSs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s10:policy_keys12:lane-budgetss4:texts1974:LANE PRIORITY AND THE LANE BUDGET — law travels faster than traffic (design/35 §3, owner-ruled 2026-07-25; design/36 K10). Law-family records are processed and delivered ahead of ordinary records in BOTH directions, and lane membership is a property of the record's own class read from the record — a record rides the law lane because its payload declares a rule_id, never because a list somewhere names its verb. An overturn rides ORDINARY: it is a DECISION, and the sweep rather than the lane is its urgency. The store's total order is untouched by any of this: the lane changes the SCHEDULING of processing, never record_time's authority (design/21, design/35 §5). PROPOSED DEFAULT, awaiting the owner's values (wall primitive 8, priced with design/23's levers). `cycle` is the number of deliveries one drain cycle may serve, and a cycle is ONE APPEND — never a tick and never a second, so the whole schedule is a function of the record's own order (the 15.1 clock ban). `reserve` is the slice of every cycle that ONLY the lane named first in `order` may spend; it is what lets a law delivery leave from behind an ordinary backlog of any depth, and with it set to zero the law lane competes in arrival order and starves under a standing flood. `depth` caps how many deliveries may wait on a lane; over the cap the oldest waiting delivery on that lane is SHED, citing this rule. A shed drops a DERIVED FLOW and never a fact — the act stays on the record permanently, and both the shed and its reason are computed rather than appended, because under a flood the sheds are exactly proportional to the flood and appending one record each would make recording scale with operation (I9). No law lane is ever shed; the reserve exists so it never has to be. FAIL-OPEN ON DELIVERY: with this policy absent from the record nothing is capped and nothing is shed, which is the pre-EP-27 behaviour exactly — there is no code-resident default standing in for a missing law.s5:scopes10:space:roots5:valued4:s5:cyclei64;s5:depthd1:s8:ordinaryi4096;s5:orderl2:s3:laws8:ordinarys7:reserved1:s3:lawi8;s7:rule_ids12:LANE-BUDGETSs9:record_ids6:rec_53d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.089137+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.089158+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.089152+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi54;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects12:DEV-LAW-BINDs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys228:the require_prior checks on BIND-DEVICE (a bind cites a registered capability) and on UNBIND (an unbind cites a prior bind), and the gate's closure step, which refuses and records any act class this law founds no declaration fors11:enforcements4:lives4:texts2426:THE DEVICE BINDING LAW. Binding a device to a driver is a recorded DECISION that cites a registered driver capability, and unbinding is a recorded decision that cites a prior bind. SEEDED as a record here at EP-29 W1a so that no device definition and no device refusal path cites a rule name the founding does not contain: BIND-DEVICE and UNBIND have cited this id since campaign 1 while no record declared it. WHAT A BIND GRANTS, STATED SO NOTHING HAS TO INFER IT: a bind grants CUSTODY of the device and NOT I/O. Custody is the right to be the bound driver; it is not admission to move bytes. I/O ADMISSION CITES A WINDOW DECLARATION BY NAME, and a window is declared under this law. AND THE WINDOW-ABSENT CASE IS DECLARED HERE RATHER THAN DEFAULTED: where this law declares no window for an act, the act is PERMITTED BY NOTHING, so the gate REFUSES it and RECORDS the refusal citing the tip-level terminal rule that an act class no declaration founds does not exist. That sentence is written rather than left to an engine default on purpose — a default filling a declaration gap with permission would be a second source of truth about what this world's law declares, and this law admits none. A REGISTRATION THAT ENUMERATES NO CAPABILITIES ENUMERATES NOTHING, and containment over it therefore permits nothing; a capability is what it names, never what it omits. THE WINDOW DECLARATIONS HAVE LANDED (EP-29 W1b, founding 1.20.0). They were held back until their surfaces were established at the guest, because a window declared before its surfaces are established is law authored against a world nobody has read. The surfaces were established by driving at the guest on 2026-08-12: three notification surfaces, of which device@sysfs and device@uevent see device APPEARANCE and BINDS and DISCLAIM device I/O in their own coverage, and arrival@tracepoints — the one whose class IS I/O arrivals — carries the subject and is readable ONLY BY A PRIVILEGED OBSERVER. So the windows declared under this law cite arrival@tracepoints and NAME THAT PRIVILEGE AS A COST rather than hiding it, and they do not borrow a reachable surface for a subject it disclaims. WHAT IS PERMITTED IS NOW EXACTLY WHAT THE DECLARED WINDOWS NAME AND NOTHING ELSE: an act class this law founds no declaration for is still permitted by nothing and still refuses, recorded, citing the tip-level terminal rule. The refusal was NARROWED, never removed.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids12:DEV-LAW-BINDs9:record_ids6:rec_54d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.091192+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.091208+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.091205+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi55;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects14:DEV-LAW-INTAKEs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys283:the require_prior check on DECLARE-INTAKE (an intake declares a grade for arrivals admitted through a DECLARED window, so a window name no record founds is refused), and the gate's closure step, which refuses and records any device intake act class this law founds no declaration fors11:enforcements4:lives4:texts4571:THE DEVICE INTAKE LAW — AT WHAT GRANULARITY AN ADMITTED DEVICE INPUT ARRIVAL IS RECORDED. DEV-LAW-BIND governs ADMISSION: a bind grants custody and not I/O, and I/O admission cites a window declaration by name. THIS law governs the RECORD: how many admitted arrivals one recorded INPUT covers. It is DECLARED, never defaulted, and a declaration under it is made by DECLARE-INTAKE citing this law. 

THE NEVER-CLAUSE, WHICH IS THIS LAW'S POINT AND IS LAW TEXT RATHER THAN COMMENTARY: AN ADMITTED INPUT ARRIVAL IS NEVER RECORDED AT PER-INTERRUPT GRADE. Interrupts arrive at device rate and the record does not. A recorded INPUT covers a WINDOW of arrivals and cites the intake declaration that bounds that window. NO DECLARATION UNDER THIS LAW MAY SET A BOUND OF ONE, at any load and for any device class: a covering record standing for exactly one arrival is the per-interrupt grade wearing a window's name. 

WHAT A COVERING RECORD CARRIES, so the never-clause costs no visibility and CONST-RECORDING-TOTAL's floor is met rather than argued around: the COUNT of arrivals covered, the BYTE TOTAL over them, the device the arrivals were attributed to, the window they were admitted under by name, and this law's citation together with the intake declaration's name. NEVER THE CONTENT — device I/O content is STREAM and is classified out, which the window declarations already state in their own does_not_see. A covering record makes no governed act invisible: it names how many there were and how many bytes they moved. 

THE GRANULARITY VALUES ARE MEASURED AND NOT ASSERTED. Every numeric value in a declaration under this law names the calibration cell it derives from, that cell's POPULATION and its BASIS, and the DERIVATION that picks the number rather than a neighbouring one (charter §A51). THREE CONSTRAINTS ON THE DERIVATION, each established by measurement at EP-29 W2a on 2026-08-13 and each written here because a later author will otherwise re-take the wrong column: 
(1) A VALUE DERIVES FROM THE ARRIVAL COUNT, NEVER FROM AN ARRIVAL RATE. Identical commands repeated three times gave arrival COUNTS at max/min ratio 1.0000 on every block cell and RATES at ratio up to 1.6265 — a rate's denominator is the window's own elapsed time, and at these window sizes that measures scheduling. 
(2) A VALUE STANDS ON DELIVERED LOAD, NEVER ON REQUESTED LOAD. A command's own number is not a population: `dd count=256` against a 366 KiB device delivered 92. Where a load tool reports no delivery figure, NO per-act value may be derived at all, and the declaration states the value UNESTABLISHED with its reason rather than dividing by the request. 
(3) A VALUE IS DECLARED PER DEVICE CLASS. Block held exactly 1.0 interrupts and exactly 3.0 arrivals per delivered request across every cell of every run, while net's interrupt-per-datagram ratio DOUBLED between runs of the same command on the same boot. One value spanning both classes would be fitted to two behaviours. 

CLOSERS ARE EVENT-BASED AND NEVER TIME-BASED, and that is FORCED rather than preferred: a duration would have to be derived from the rate column constraint (1) bars, and a clock that schedules a record is clock-as-scheduler, which this estate admits only as clock-as-sensor (TICK-POLICY). 

FAIL-CLOSED, IN THIS GROUP'S OWN DIRECTION AND DELIBERATELY NOT THE FILES GROUP'S. FS-WRITE-COALESCING fails closed by recording MORE — absent the policy, every write appends its own covering decision. THAT DIRECTION IS UNAVAILABLE HERE, because recording every arrival IS the per-interrupt grade this law forbids: the files group's safe direction is this group's forbidden one. So the device direction is DEV-LAW-BIND's own — where this law declares NO intake for a device class, arrivals of that class are ADMITTED BY NOTHING, the intake refuses, and the refusal is RECORDED citing the tip-level terminal rule that an act class no declaration founds does not exist. Nothing goes dark: the refusal is the record. 

WHAT THIS LAW DOES NOT DO. It declares the GRADE and does not build the intake ENGINE: recording at the declared grade through the gate is the shim's contract (EP-29 W3), and the pass that lands this law makes no product record under it. And the declarations under it hold for the validation set on the lab guest at the load levels that guest was driven at; what population licenses them for another device, another guest or a higher load is NOT settled here, is named in each declaration's own limits, and is carried to the campaign close as an open question rather than assumed.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids14:DEV-LAW-INTAKEs9:record_ids6:rec_55d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.093313+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.093331+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.093327+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi56;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects14:MEM-LAW-BUDGETs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys418:the `ceiling` check on MEM-GRANT (a grant is admitted only while the holder's resident fold plus the requested size stays under the recorded `budget:{holder}` ceiling; over-budget refuses, recorded, citing this rule), the shape leash that refuses a bare summable ceiling declaration at all three definition doors including the founding, and AMEND-BUDGET's decisions which cite this law when they set a holder's ceilings11:enforcements4:lives4:texts3319:THE MEMORY BUDGET LAW — A GRANT OF RESIDENT SPACE IS ADMITTED ONLY WHILE THE HOLDER'S RESIDENT TOTAL STAYS UNDER ITS CEILING. A per-holder budget is recorded policy set by AMEND-BUDGET (policy_key `budget:{holder}`), and a grant of resident space (MEM-GRANT) is admitted only while the holder's already-granted total plus the requested size stays at or under that ceiling. The check that enforces this is `ceiling`, which cites this law; a grant that would exceed the ceiling is REFUSED, recorded, citing this rule (the rule-errno pack maps it to ENOMEM). A holder with NO recorded ceiling is unlimited — the pre-budget behaviour stated in the law rather than filled in by an engine default. SEEDED as a record here so that MEM-GRANT's ceiling refusal and AMEND-BUDGET's decisions no longer cite a rule name the founding does not contain (the containment line): both have cited this id while no record declared it, so until now their every budget refusal would have named nothing. 

THE USED-TOTAL IS A FOLD, NEVER A STORED MAGNITUDE, AND THAT IS THIS LAW'S §A51 RESOLUTION. The ceiling compares the requested size against a SUM — the holder's resident total — and charter §A51 forbids a summable value that does not carry its POPULATION and its BASIS. This law resolves that inside its own wording rather than by minting a new kind of check: the used-total is COMPUTED FROM THE RECORD ON EVERY EVALUATION AND IS NEVER STORED, so it carries its population and basis BY CONSTRUCTION — a re-derived fold cannot lose the provenance a stored copy would, because the population is the set of records it folds and the basis is the field it reads from each. ITS POPULATION is the holder's currently-resident regions: every MEM-GRANT decision scoped to this holder (`aggregate_action` MEM-GRANT, `holder_field` holder) that a later MEM-EVICT has not removed (`removal_action` MEM-EVICT, unfiltered by holder because an eviction frees whatever region it names), keyed by region (`key_param` region) so a re-grant OVERWRITES its region's contribution rather than double-counting it. ITS BASIS is each such region's recorded size (`aggregate_field` size). These are the CEILING ROW'S OWN DECLARED FIELDS — the population and the basis §A51 requires are NAMED IN THE DECLARATION, never hidden inside a scalar — and the sum is an ORDERED KEYED FOLD over the whole record so grant / evict / re-grant interleave correctly. A ceiling declaration that summed a field WITHOUT declaring this population and this basis would be a BARE SUMMABLE, which violates §A51; this law does not admit it and the founding door refuses it. 

WHAT THIS LAW DOES NOT DO. It declares the MEASURE and mints NO new check kind: `ceiling` is the existing kind that reads and sums a numeric field of prior records, and this law states how that sum stays §A51-honest rather than adding a new operator to the vocabulary. Its sibling memory laws — the allocation law MEM-GRANT cites (MEM-LAW-ALLOC), the eviction law MEM-EVICT cites (MEM-LAW-EVICT), and the protection law MEM-PROTECT cites (MEM-LAW-PROTECT) — are cited-but-uncreated and scheduled at this unit; each is a founding act awaiting the owner's word and is NOT created by this law's create. This budget law's own create is the one the owner granted; the family does not ride it.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids14:MEM-LAW-BUDGETs9:record_ids6:rec_56d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.095307+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.095323+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.095320+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi57;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects12:KEY-LAW-BINDs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys657:the gate anchor guard (kernel.gate): a HANDOVER refuses a keyless successor when the chain-end is key-bound (the succession key half, engaging PER-ACCOUNT on key_valid of the chain-end), and a root-key key-rotate/key-revoke refuses without a successor key bound through the recorded human-only succession ceremony (is_root_key_op discriminates the root key; a non-root key op is unleashed); the live validity fold in kernel.keys (bound_key / key_valid, latest-supersession-wins, as-of supported) that the key operations write into and the signed door will read (EP-37); and the vault-class custody consumed from CONST-SECRETS (seal write-once, no read path)s11:enforcements4:lives4:texts2159:THE KEY-BIND LAW. A verification anchor binds an account to a public key by a recorded ceremony act; rotation and revocation are SUPERSESSIONS evaluated LIVE (latest-supersession-wins), never a stored validity — nothing cascades on revocation because nothing downstream is stored (theorem 7 applied to keys, DIGEST-C2 §2). The root key is founding-asserted for the chain-end and nothing sits above it to verify against (design/37 Q6; J1's self-aware limit); the system key sits in VAULT-CLASS custody (the secrets vault, consumed unchanged, CONST-SECRETS) with NO read path — the store's countersignature is possession-in-custody plus minted provenance, never a decrypt-and-sign, because a key held in closure cannot be read to sign with. SEEDED as a record here so that the key operations (key-bind / key-rotate / key-revoke), the succession key half, and the root-key anchor no longer engage against a rule name the founding does not contain: this law's PRESENCE is the wire-at-birth gate (key_law_live) that engages them, and its ABSENCE leaves every world founded before it with exactly the succession behaviour it had before. THE ENGAGE IS PER-ACCOUNT, NOT ESTATE-WIDE (the EP-36 engage amendment): the succession key half refuses a keyless successor ONLY when the chain-end account is ITSELF key-bound (key_valid of the chain-end); a keyless account hands over on the succession fold alone, forever, until it binds, so declaring this law flag-days no account and reds no settled keyless handover. NO NEW CHECK KIND is minted: key validity is a supersession value-fold (kernel.keys), the succession/secret precedent, not a new member of the closed check vocabulary. The root key's leash is CONST-AUTHORITY-ANCHORED in cryptographic form — the same root-never-orphans law the succession ceremony already carries, now over the root KEY. This law's own create is the one the owner granted (board :2847, 'create the key family: bind/rotate/revoke, and the two per-stream audit keys'); no sibling law rides it, and no real key is minted by declaring it (the real ceremony that binds an owner/system/successor key is the owner's, design/37 §11 item 6).s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids12:KEY-LAW-BINDs9:record_ids6:rec_57d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.097194+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.097211+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.097206+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi58;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects20:AUDIT-KEY-DUAL-AUDITs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys272:the per-stream signing path kernel.protection._sign_stream_a, which names ONLY this stream's key (the key-path separation guard proves neither signing path reaches the other's key), and the vault-class custody consumed from the key-bind law (seal write-once, no read path)s11:enforcements4:lives4:texts1362:THE PER-STREAM AUDIT KEY — STREAM dual-audit. The first of the two audit streams (kernel.protection dual-audit / dual-audit-b) signs its mirror records with its OWN key, held in VAULT-CLASS custody (seal write-once, NO read path — the closure consumed from the key-bind law, never a decrypt-and-sign). Verification is DERIVED from the public custody hash; holding this stream's key produces THIS stream's signatures ALONE and can neither produce nor forge the other stream's, so a consistent signed pair requires BOTH keys (design/37 Q4, §9 the custody cap; the EP-09 physical-separation deferral landed as key separation). The two keys are SEPARABLE BY CONFIGURATION (configuration-as-record, a named-owner calibration): on a single-owner box both keys may still sit with one person and that is lawful — the property delivered is that the streams CAN have separate custodians with the checking machinery unchanged, the deployment picks custodians. SEEDED as a record here with its sibling AUDIT-KEY-DUAL-AUDIT-B so that the founding names both per-stream key homes; this create is the owner's word of board :2847 ('the two per-stream audit keys'), named as its own element and never ridden silently under the key family. No real key material is minted by declaring it — the real ceremony that seals the two custody homes is the owner's (design/37 §9).s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids20:AUDIT-KEY-DUAL-AUDITs9:record_ids6:rec_58d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.101830+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.101878+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.101869+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi59;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects22:AUDIT-KEY-DUAL-AUDIT-Bs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys272:the per-stream signing path kernel.protection._sign_stream_b, which names ONLY this stream's key (the key-path separation guard proves neither signing path reaches the other's key), and the vault-class custody consumed from the key-bind law (seal write-once, no read path)s11:enforcements4:lives4:texts965:THE PER-STREAM AUDIT KEY — STREAM dual-audit-b. The second of the two audit streams (kernel.protection dual-audit / dual-audit-b) signs its mirror records with its OWN key, held in VAULT-CLASS custody (seal write-once, NO read path — the closure consumed from the key-bind law, never a decrypt-and-sign). Verification is DERIVED from the public custody hash; holding this stream's key produces THIS stream's signatures ALONE and can neither produce nor forge the other stream's, so a consistent signed pair requires BOTH keys (design/37 Q4, §9 the custody cap). Separable by configuration as its sibling AUDIT-KEY-DUAL-AUDIT states; both keys may sit with one custodian on a single-owner box (lawful) and the property delivered is that they CAN separate with the checking machinery unchanged. SEEDED here as its own named element of the owner's board :2847 word; no real key material is minted by declaring it (the real ceremony is the owner's, design/37 §9).s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids22:AUDIT-KEY-DUAL-AUDIT-Bs9:record_ids6:rec_59d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.104146+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.104163+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.104159+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi60;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects8:SIGN-LAWs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys556:the gate acceptance step's signed door (kernel.gate._signed_door, EP-37 W1): a key-bound account's act with no valid signature from a key valid as of the act's deciding time is REFUSED, recorded, citing this rule; the door reads kernel.keys.bound_key (the EP-36 supersession fold, as-of the deciding time) LIVE per act and keeps no signer state between acts, so its reach grows per-account as keys bind and the account with no bound key is never entered; the invoking account's signature is recorded in provenance (design/37 Q2), never a new envelope fields11:enforcements4:lives4:texts2779:THE SIGNING LAW. For an account that holds a bound verification key, the gate accepts no act without a valid signature over the act, from a key valid AS OF the act's deciding time (the key-validity fold in kernel.keys, latest-supersession-wins, read live at every check). An account with NO bound key acts exactly as it does today under the founding openness grant — declaring this law narrows no one on its own, and with no key bound anywhere the whole pre-existing behaviour is byte-identical (the zero-keys world changes nobody). THE FLIP IS PER-ACCOUNT, NOT ESTATE-WIDE and never a flag day: BINDING A KEY IS THE NARROWING ACT (design/37 Q6, the campaign crux R20-2 — the caller-asserted actor carried by name since campaign 1 retires at the cryptographic door, per-account); there is no dual-mode gate and no compatibility flag. The retirement of caller-asserted acceptance for keyed accounts is LICENSED by the EP-17 strictly-stronger proof on the extended authorization ledger (an old ledger cannot contain cases in the signature dimension it could not express) — emitted before the retirement stands, a naked swap is a rejected diff. SEEDED as a record here so the gate's signed door no longer engages against a rule name the founding does not contain: this law's PRESENCE is the wire-at-birth gate (SIGN-LAW in active_rules) that engages the door, and its ABSENCE leaves every world founded before it acting exactly as it did. NO NEW CHECK KIND is minted — the acceptance step reads the existing key-validity fold and verifies a signature derived on demand (possession-in-custody plus minted provenance, the keys.countersign shape); it is enforcement mechanics, ORDINARY/OWNER-TIER and NOT constitutional (constitutional law is genesis-only entrenchment in this estate; the legitimacy anchor is the founding and the root keys the door READS, never the door itself, so the law must stay amendable — its reach grows per-account by ordinary key-binds and its parameters are D4 calibrations). TWO WRONG REFERENCES REFUSED: the SESSION/TOKEN — a login minting a token, a 'current signer' cache, an authenticated-session the gate consults — each stores identity STATUS between acts and is refused by there being no cache to consult (the account is resolved anew and the signature verified LIVE, every act); and the BLOCKCHAIN/PKI PAIR — consensus where there is one sovereign store, certificate chains where verification bottoms out at the founding — refused by there being no trust store and no primitive minted. This law's own create is the one the owner granted (board :2847 'create the signing law', ordinary owner-tier per :2788); no real key is minted by declaring it — the first real key ever bound is the owner's own act (design/37 §11, EP-37 W4).s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids8:SIGN-LAWs9:record_ids6:rec_60d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.108970+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.109001+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.108994+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi61;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects13:PLACEMENT-LAWs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys437:kernel.opdefs run: a param declared in content_params is written to gate.blobs (content-addressed put) and the record keeps the returned hash under the declared field, never the interior bytes (the built mint, opdefs content_params); no size threshold is consulted on this path — structural_field_classification / _content_home_params / _require_field_classification carry no size token, driven by tests/test_ep41a.py TestNoSizeNumber.s11:enforcements4:lives4:texts1444:THE PLACEMENT LAW (design/46 member 1). A payload field an op definition DECLARES a content-interior — content_params (blob store) or secret_params (vault) — lives OUTSIDE the row as content-addressed bytes: the bytes go to the store keyed by their own hash and the row keeps ONLY that hash, never the interior. The routing criterion is the op definition's OWN declaration and nothing else — NO SIZE NUMBER exists, is read, or ever enters (design/46 §2.1: a number is friction pretending to be coverage; the struck size floor stays struck). Custody of an interior is thereby conservable: the bytes have ONE home, addressed by content, so a handover or departure acts on that home rather than on a value smeared inline across the record. SEEDED as a record here so the built placement mechanism (opdefs run: content_params -> gate.blobs.put, the row records the hash) is DECLARED law rather than an undeclared engine behaviour; its create is the owner's fourth serial mover (board :2870 item 4). NO NEW CHECK KIND and NO NEW RECORD KIND are minted — placement is the built content_params mint, now lawed. TWO WRONG REFERENCES REFUSED: the SIZE FLOOR (keep small interiors inline, blob the large) — refused by there being no size read anywhere on the placement path; and the INLINE COPY (a row carrying both the hash and the bytes) — refused by the row keeping the hash alone, so an interior has exactly one custody-conservable home.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids13:PLACEMENT-LAWs9:record_ids6:rec_61d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.113753+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.113785+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.113777+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi62;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects14:VOCAB-DOOR-LAWs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys426:kernel.opdefs.validate_definition_shape._require_field_classification, engaged when the vocabulary-door law is live (classify_law_live): the founding installer reads it from the pack's own rule records (founding.install._validate), and CREATE-OP / AMEND-OP read VOCAB-DOOR-LAW in views.active_rules(); a definition with any unclassified payload field is refused under AR-2 naming the vocabulary door, at all three doors alike.s11:enforcements4:lives4:texts2230:THE VOCABULARY DOOR (design/46 member 2). No op definition enters the founding, or the registry through CREATE-OP or AMEND-OP, unless EVERY payload field it declares is classified — STRUCTURAL (structural_params: the author affirms the field carries no reachable content and is safe to live inline) or CONTENT (content_params / secret_params: the value is blob-homed by hash and never lands inline, per the placement law). A declared field in NEITHER class is UNCLASSIFIED and the whole definition is REFUSED, because silence about a field is exactly the collision this door closes: content living inline where custody could never be conserved for it. The completeness check runs in ONE place — kernel.opdefs.validate_definition_shape — at all THREE doors (the founding installer, CREATE-OP, AMEND-OP), so a guard at two of three doors is not what this is. It DISCRIMINATES and does not over-block: a fully-classified definition is admitted; the door's question is completeness of classification, never whether a field DECLARED structural in fact carries content (that residual is auditable by census, design/46 §4, not this door). SEEDED as a record here so the door engages: this law's PRESENCE is the wire-at-birth gate (VOCAB-DOOR-LAW in active_rules at the runtime doors; the pack's own rule record at the founding door), and its ABSENCE leaves every world founded before it classifying nothing and founding exactly as it did. A one-time classification sweep of the DRIVEN op census (install.op_definitions, never a named list) rides this create so the whole existing population passes the door. Its create is the owner's fourth serial mover (board :2870 item 4). NO NEW CHECK KIND is minted — completeness reads the definition's own declarations and refuses under the existing shape rule AR-2; a forced new check kind would have been a STOP, not this law. TWO WRONG REFERENCES REFUSED: the ACT-LAYER SCAN (inspect each act's payload at execution for stray content) — refused by catching classification at the RULE layer, at the definition, before any act; and the SIZE HEURISTIC (classify a field by how big its value tends to be) — refused by classification being the author's DECLARATION, never a measurement.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids14:VOCAB-DOOR-LAWs9:record_ids6:rec_62d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.123760+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.123785+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.123779+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi63;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects16:RECOVER-LAW-GS14s6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd4:s4:kinds4:rules4:texts290:recover: on damage, the root authority holder may splice the record to an OLDER SOUND point the chain adjudicates as intact, bringing verified segments in, verified AT LOAD; it appends a recover record, rewrites and deletes nothing, and never installs a state the record never lawfully helds5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids16:RECOVER-LAW-GS14s9:record_ids6:rec_63d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.126224+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.126247+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.126242+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi64;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects19:RECOVERY-CAPACITY-1s6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd7:s18:permits_truncationb0s18:recover_disciplines11:append-onlys4:kinds4:rules4:texts475:recovery capacity = content redundancy × relational redundancy: relations ADJUDICATE (localize damage, constrain the missing piece, verify candidates) and anchors SUPPLY (replicas, firmware copy, full-fidelity projections — the living dialects); neither alone recovers; a recover APPENDS a recover row and NEVER removes, rewrites, or shortens the record; AND a sealed piece with NO living reader key is 'sealed forever' — custody-conserved but NEVER counted recoverable.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids19:RECOVERY-CAPACITY-1s8:coveragel2:d3:s10:relationals43:the hash chain + Merkle-shaped localizations7:contents50:content-addressed blobs (replicas / firmware copy)s9:subsystems5:filesd3:s10:relationals66:the record's derivation relations (fold from LAW+DECISIONS+INPUTS)s7:contents51:the full-fidelity projection pinned at a checkpoints9:subsystems6:memorys9:record_ids6:rec_64d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.135789+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.135829+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.135820+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi65;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects17:COMM-LAW-CONTRACTs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys784:COMMS-OPEN's own check rows THAT CITE THIS LAW — the `value_domain` on role (system-facing / user-facing) and the `live_slot` two-channel invariant (one live opening per entity per role, opens vs closes) — AND NOTHING ELSE YET. COMMS-OPEN also carries a `require_prior` over the entity against CREATE-ACCOUNT, but that is the entity-existence check every crossing op carries and it cites CAP-IS-LAW, not this rule — this law does not claim it. The CLOSE path is unenforced (COMMS-CLOSE carries no check row); the channel-liveness-at-close and whose-channel questions are scheduled to a later comms unit, the create preceding their enforcement. The channel-holding fold in src/subsystems/comms.py reads the COMMS-OPEN / COMMS-CLOSE record alone and holds no stored channel table.s11:enforcements4:lives4:texts2127:THE CHANNEL CONTRACT LAW. A channel is an OPENING OF AN ENTITY, held in exactly one of two declared roles — system-facing or user-facing (design/39 §6-7; a third role was refused by name, so the role domain is settled law refusing, never a type error). An open (COMMS-OPEN) and a close (COMMS-CLOSE) are each a recorded, rule-citing decision; the open MINTS the channel object and the close tears it down. THE TWO-CHANNEL INVARIANT: an entity holds AT MOST ONE LIVE channel per role at a moment, counted as LIVE OPENINGS — opens not yet closed — never openings across a lifetime (design/39 §3); a second live open in a role the entity already holds is REFUSED, recorded, citing this rule. The named entity must be one A PRIOR RECORD ESTABLISHED (a channel is an opening of somebody's, and the somebody must exist on the record), never a string the caller supplies. WHO HOLDS WHICH CHANNEL NOW IS DERIVED, never stored: a fold over these open/close decisions (src/subsystems/comms.py live_channels, keyed by (entity, channel)), so killing every derived structure and replaying the record alone returns the same answer. SEEDED as a record here so that COMMS-OPEN's role and live-channel refusals and COMMS-CLOSE's decision no longer cite a rule name the founding does not contain (the EP-25 ADDENDUM 4 containment line): these operations have cited this rule since they shipped and the pack has never held it, so until now their every refusal would have named nothing. WHAT THIS RULE DOES NOT YET REACH, STATED IN THE LAW ITSELF SO NO READER INFERS COVER IT DOES NOT HAVE: the CLOSE path carries no check and is NAME-KEYED — COMMS-CLOSE carries only `channel`, so a close drops every live opening with that name and does NOT ask whose channel it closes nor whether a live opening exists to close; a close of an unopened or already-closed channel is recorded, not refused (the finding src/subsystems/comms.py already raises at its live_channels fold). The close path's enforcement is scheduled, the create preceding it. A LAW THAT CLAIMED THE COVER IT DOES NOT HAVE WOULD BE WORSE THAN THE DANGLING CITATION IT REPLACES.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids17:COMM-LAW-CONTRACTs9:record_ids6:rec_65d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.142932+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.142975+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.142967+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi66;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects14:COMM-LAW-QUEUEs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys506:NOTHING YET, and the law's own text says so: COMMS-SEND and COMMS-RECV carry no check row today, so no refusal path enforces this rule at present — its create precedes its enforcement. Its PRESENCE cures the containment line (a COMMS-SEND / COMMS-RECV decision now cites a rule the founding contains); the liveness, non-empty, ordering and authority checks are scheduled to a later comms unit. The queue-state fold in src/subsystems/comms.py reads the send/receive record alone and holds no stored queue.s11:enforcements4:lives4:texts2000:THE MESSAGE QUEUE LAW. A message crosses a channel by two recorded, rule-citing decisions: an ENQUEUE (COMMS-SEND) that records the message CONTENT-ADDRESSED (the payload carried as its hash, full-fidelity content in the content store, the event recording the hash) and a DEQUEUE (COMMS-RECV) that records the taking. THE QUEUE STATE IS DERIVED, never stored: a fold over a channel's send and receive decisions (src/subsystems/comms.py, "the queue state and the message ledger are derived views"), so killing every derived structure and replaying the record alone returns the same pending set. SEEDED as a record here so that COMMS-SEND and COMMS-RECV no longer cite a rule name the founding does not contain (the EP-25 ADDENDUM 4 containment line): both have cited this rule since they shipped and the pack has never held it, so until now any decision under them would have named a rule the record did not carry. WHAT THIS RULE DOES NOT YET REACH, STATED IN THE LAW ITSELF SO NO READER INFERS COVER IT DOES NOT HAVE — AND IT IS MORE THAN THE SIBLING SEEDS OWE, BECAUSE THESE OPS ARE CHECK-LESS TODAY: COMMS-SEND and COMMS-RECV carry NO CHECK ROW (checks: []). So this law's PRESENCE cures the containment (a decision under them now cites a rule the founding contains) while its ENFORCEMENT is entirely ahead of it. It does NOT ask whether the channel is LIVE before a send or a receive (a send on a closed or never-opened channel is recorded, not refused); it does NOT ask whether the queue is NON-EMPTY before a receive (a receive on an empty queue is recorded, not refused); it enforces NO ordering, NO delivery-once, and NO sender/receiver authority over the channel. Each is unenforced today and the law says so at its own hand rather than leaving a reader to discover it: the create precedes its enforcement, and the check vocabulary that would ask these questions is scheduled, not skipped. A LAW THAT CLAIMED THE COVER IT DOES NOT HAVE WOULD BE WORSE THAN THE DANGLING CITATION IT REPLACES.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids14:COMM-LAW-QUEUEs9:record_ids6:rec_66d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.147757+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.147787+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.147780+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi67;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects13:NET-LAW-GRANTs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys663:SOCKET-OPEN's own check rows THAT CITE THIS LAW — the require_prior over the opener's establishment (WHO) and the value_domain over the address family (WHAT) — AND the require_prior over SOCKET-OPEN that SOCKET-BIND / SOCKET-LISTEN / SOCKET-CONNECT / SOCKET-ACCEPT / SOCKET-CLOSE each carry (a wire act names a socket the record opened). The WHERE (a connect/accept target) is recorded and UNGATED today — no reach check over the target is carried; that enforcement is scheduled to EP-48G / EP-52 (the firewall), the create preceding it. The socket-table fold in src/subsystems/sockets.py reads the six wire decisions alone and holds no stored socket table.s11:enforcements4:lives4:texts1830:THE SOCKET GRANT LAW (EP-48, design/51 §5). Who may open what, to where — the six wire acts are AUTHORITY against the record, never a re-statement of the POSIX call (the wrong reference this unit names). A socket is OPENED BY an established entity (WHO — SOCKET-OPEN's require_prior over the opener's establishment); the socket's family is one this law declares (WHAT — SOCKET-OPEN's value_domain over the address family: inet, inet6, unix; a family this law does not name is REFUSED, recorded, citing this rule, never a type error); a bind/listen/connect/accept/close acts on a socket THE RECORD OPENED (a wire act names a socket somebody's record established, never a string the caller supplies — each carries a require_prior over SOCKET-OPEN). The live socket table is DERIVED, never stored: a fold over these six wire decisions (src/subsystems/sockets.py), so killing every derived structure and replaying the record alone returns the same table. THE GOVERNED CONNECTION VIEW IS THIS FOLD: the observe-plane connection shadow (observe/shadow.py m1-connection-state) retires INTO it once the fold reproduces the shadow's mature diff-empty under the churn battery, both directions (:2646 paid). WHAT THIS RULE DOES NOT YET REACH, STATED IN THE LAW ITSELF SO NO READER INFERS COVER IT DOES NOT HAVE: the WHERE a connect/accept names (the target/peer) is RECORDED but not gated to a declared reach today — no value_domain nor grant over the target is carried, so a connect to any target by an entity that may open the family is recorded, not refused; and on the HOST no real socket is opened by the kernel port — the wire is a MODELLED table (the guest-real half is EP-48G, where a refused grant is a real socket refused + errno). A LAW THAT CLAIMED THE COVER IT DOES NOT HAVE WOULD BE WORSE THAN A DANGLING CITATION.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids13:NET-LAW-GRANTs9:record_ids6:rec_67d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.162868+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.162895+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.162889+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi68;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-RULEs6:objects14:NET-LAW-FILTERs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd5:s11:enforced_bys896:FILTER-DECISION's own check rows THAT CITE THIS LAW — the require_prior over CREATE-RULE (the cited FILTER-RULE must exist in the record; a decision citing no rule is refused), the require_prior over SOCKET-OPEN (the decision is over a connection the record established, not a string the caller supplies), the value_domain over the decision (admit | refuse) and the value_domain over the seam (guest-netfilter — the record names the enforcement seam and never lets the host claim the drop). The which-rule-decided census is a FOLD in src/subsystems/filter.py reading the FILTER-DECISION acts alone (never a socket act, never a channel act — the wire/channel/filter folds are disjoint by construction, N2). The guest kernel's netfilter is the enforcement layer the seam names, exercised guest-only under the pinned kernel; the host record decides and names the seam and drops no real packet.s11:enforcements4:lives4:texts2137:THE FIREWALL LAW (EP-52, design/51 N8 / §4-5-9). The firewall is RULES IN THE RECORD, not a table beside it — the wrong reference this unit names is iptables as the law: a cache with no record of WHY a connection was refused, so the census of 'which rule decided' is impossible. A FILTER-RULE is a CREATE-RULE of this law class (NET-LAW-FILTER, the NET-LAW-GRANT precedent); a FILTER-DECISION act CITES one and decides over the socket table (EP-48, src/subsystems/sockets.py) — a connection ADMITTED or REFUSED, WITH the deciding rule recorded, so 'which rule dropped this' is a FOLD over the FILTER-DECISION acts (src/subsystems/filter.py) and never an inference. A FILTER-DECISION that cites NO rule the record holds is REFUSED, recorded, citing this law: the firewall is law in the record, and a decision that cites nothing is not a decision. The decision is over a connection SOMEBODY'S RECORD ESTABLISHED (a require_prior over SOCKET-OPEN — the filter READS and decides OVER the socket table, it never rewrites a socket act). ROUTING AND NAME RESOLUTION SPLIT POLICY FROM INPUT (design/44 §2): where the system DECIDES (admit or refuse a name or route by policy) it is a FILTER-DECISION citing this law; where the world HANDS US A FACT (a resolver answer, a route) it is INPUT — the clock-as-sensor law generalized to every oracle (ANSWER-RETURNED), never a decision the record authors. THE ENFORCEMENT SEAM IS NAMED, GUEST-ONLY: the record DECIDES; the guest kernel's netfilter DROPS the real packet; a FILTER-DECISION NAMES its seam (guest-netfilter) and the host record NEVER claims the drop — the same honesty as the disk lock under the modelled-key cap (DIGEST-C4 §6). WHAT THIS LAW DOES NOT REACH, STATED SO NO READER INFERS COVER IT DOES NOT HAVE: per-packet traffic is NEVER a record — STREAM appends nothing, the firewall decides connections and names the seam; and on the HOST no real packet is dropped by the record — the drop is the GUEST kernel's netfilter, proven guest-only under the pinned kernel (EP-00 rule 9). A LAW THAT CLAIMED THE COVER IT DOES NOT HAVE WOULD BE WORSE THAN A DANGLING CITATION.s5:scopes10:space:roots7:rule_ids14:NET-LAW-FILTERs9:record_ids6:rec_68d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.171254+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.171276+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.171271+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi70;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-INFOs6:objects19:pack:venn2-geometrys6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd3:s4:kinds13:category_packs4:names14:venn2-geometrys6:levelsl5:s2:URs2:ADs2:INs2:NSs2:EMs9:record_ids6:rec_70d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.173392+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.173418+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.173412+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi71;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-INFOs6:objects20:pack:strength-levelss6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd3:s4:kinds13:category_packs4:names15:strength-levelss6:levelsl5:s8:absolutes11:significants4:fairs5:minors4:zeros9:record_ids6:rec_71d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.175417+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.175437+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.175431+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi72;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-INFOs6:objects23:pack:dual-audit-actionss6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd3:s4:kinds13:category_packs4:names18:dual-audit-actionss6:levelsl12:s12:AMEND-BUDGETs10:AMEND-RULEs10:GRANT-READs12:HALT-WATCHERs5:MOUNTs19:REGISTER-CAPABILITYs15:RESOLVE-WATCHERs10:SCHED-HALTs10:SCHED-KILLs14:VERIFY-ACCOUNTs13:VERIFY-SECRETs10:op-refuseds9:record_ids6:rec_72d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.185588+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.185614+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.185608+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi75;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-INFOs6:objects26:pack:law-lifecycle-actionss6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd3:s4:kinds13:category_packs4:names21:law-lifecycle-actionss6:levelsl8:s11:CREATE-RULEs9:CREATE-OPs8:AMEND-OPs9:RETIRE-OPs10:AMEND-PACKs17:CREATE-OBLIGATIONs11:CREATE-VIEWs11:RETIRE-RULEs9:record_ids6:rec_75d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.191505+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.191532+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.191527+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi76;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-INFOs6:objects22:pack:authority-actionss6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd3:s4:kinds13:category_packs4:names17:authority-actionss6:levelsl1:s4:holds9:record_ids6:rec_76d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.195693+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.195709+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.195707+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi77;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-INFOs6:objects17:pack:emit-actionss6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd3:s4:kinds13:category_packs4:names12:emit-actionss6:levelsl1:s4:EMITs9:record_ids6:rec_77d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.197729+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.197747+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.197744+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi78;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-INFOs6:objects22:pack:structure-actionss6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd3:s4:kinds13:category_packs4:names17:structure-actionss6:levelsl2:s12:CREATE-SPACEs11:CREATE-ROLEs9:record_ids6:rec_78d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.202610+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.202638+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.202633+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi79;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-INFOs6:objects16:pack:syscall-opss6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd4:s4:kinds13:category_packs4:names11:syscall-opss6:amendss573:the genesis pack:syscall-ops (step 06-vocabulary-packs): socket->SOCKET-OPEN replaces socket->COMMS-OPEN, recvmsg->COMMS-RECV added, bind/listen/connect/accept->their socket ops added; the wire and the channel are TWO names in law (N2). The old socket->COMMS-OPEN mapping survives in history and is never edited (append-only founding data, design/51 §6 item 3). `close` is deliberately NOT mapped: the flat map cannot discriminate a socket fd's close from a file fd's, so SOCKET-CLOSE is reached by the op directly on the host (:3266 precision 3); EP-48G maps it in-guest.s6:levelsl13:d2:s2:ops11:FILE-CREATEs7:syscalls4:opend2:s2:ops10:FILE-WRITEs7:syscalls5:writed2:s2:ops11:FILE-UNLINKs7:syscalls6:unlinkd2:s2:ops9:FILE-PERMs7:syscalls5:chmodd2:s2:ops9:MEM-GRANTs7:syscalls4:mmapd2:s2:ops5:MOUNTs7:syscalls5:mountd2:s2:ops11:SOCKET-OPENs7:syscalls6:socketd2:s2:ops11:SOCKET-BINDs7:syscalls4:bindd2:s2:ops13:SOCKET-LISTENs7:syscalls6:listend2:s2:ops14:SOCKET-CONNECTs7:syscalls7:connectd2:s2:ops13:SOCKET-ACCEPTs7:syscalls6:acceptd2:s2:ops10:COMMS-SENDs7:syscalls7:sendmsgd2:s2:ops10:COMMS-RECVs7:syscalls7:recvmsgs9:record_ids6:rec_79d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.205690+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.205715+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.205709+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi80;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-INFOs6:objects15:pack:rule-errnos6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd4:s4:kinds13:category_packs4:names10:rule-errnos6:amendss1001:the genesis pack:rule-errno (step 07-policy-vocabularies): adds NET-LAW-GRANT -> EACCES (EP-48G, design/51 N1). A refused socket grant (NET-LAW-GRANT: who may open what, to where, EP-48) is a permission denial the record decides; before this row a refused grant fell back to EINVAL, a true refusal wearing the wrong ABI answer. EACCES is the errno POSIX gives socket(2) when permission to create the socket is denied — the literal SOCKET-OPEN refusal — and it groups with this estate's other permission laws (FS-LAW-PERM, SIGHT-IS-LAW -> EACCES), distinct from EPERM (the capability class, CAP-IS-LAW). The guest-real half: a refused grant is a real socket refused + this errno + the refusal row. The genesis 7-row pack survives in history and is never edited (append-only founding data, design/51 §6 item 3); latest-wins by sequence serves this 8-row amendment. This adds a data row only — no op, no law, no check kind (op-population and OP_CHECKS unchanged); a data-born MINOR bump, attested.s6:levelsl8:d2:s4:rules14:MEM-LAW-BUDGETs5:errnos6:ENOMEMd2:s4:rules11:FS-LAW-PERMs5:errnos6:EACCESd2:s4:rules16:FS-LAW-NAMESPACEs5:errnos6:EACCESd2:s4:rules10:CAP-IS-LAWs5:errnos5:EPERMd2:s4:rules12:SIGHT-IS-LAWs5:errnos6:EACCESd2:s4:rules10:P3-CLOSUREs5:errnos6:ENOSYSd2:s4:rules10:ROOT-NEG-6s5:errnos6:EEXISTd2:s4:rules13:NET-LAW-GRANTs5:errnos6:EACCESs9:record_ids6:rec_80d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.208687+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.208714+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.208708+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi81;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-INFOs6:objects16:pack:derivationss6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd3:s4:kinds13:category_packs4:names11:derivationss6:levelsl9:s3:SELs3:LOCs3:LPKs4:LIVEs3:CNTs4:WALKs5:MATCHs10:HASH-MATCHs3:ORDs9:record_ids6:rec_81d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds8:BOOT-INTs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.210976+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.211014+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.211008+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi82;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions11:CREATE-INFOs6:objects28:pack:derivation-compositionss6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd3:s4:kinds13:category_packs4:names23:derivation-compositionss6:levelsl3:s12:every_members5:unions9:composites9:record_ids6:rec_82d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds10:CAP-IS-LAWs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.213664+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.213691+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.213685+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi83;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions9:CREATE-OPs6:objects22:op:REGISTER-CAPABILITYs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd7:s10:definitiond9:s11:descriptions395:admit a driver capability (a LAW amendment): the driver declares the device class and the capabilities it may use. A registration that enumerates no capabilities enumerates NOTHING and containment over it permits nothing (DEV-LAW-BIND) — the two enumerating parameters are declared OPTIONAL, which is a statement that a capability may be registered bare, never that a bare one covers anything.s12:object_params6:drivers12:payload_froml3:s6:drivers12:device_classs12:capabilitiess17:structural_paramsl3:s6:drivers12:device_classs12:capabilitiess4:cells1:Ss5:mintsl1:s6:drivers6:checksl0:s6:paramsd3:s12:capabilitiess8:optionals12:device_classs8:optionals6:drivers8:requireds9:law_citeds10:CAP-IS-LAWs4:kinds13:op_definitions4:names19:REGISTER-CAPABILITYs4:texts122:operation REGISTER-CAPABILITY: when invoked and its checks pass -> append a REGISTER-CAPABILITY decision citing CAP-IS-LAWs4:tiers5:owners7:rule_ids22:op:REGISTER-CAPABILITYs8:polaritys1:+s9:record_ids6:rec_83d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds10:CAP-IS-LAWs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.217907+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.217926+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.217923+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi84;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions9:CREATE-OPs6:objects14:op:BIND-DEVICEs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd7:s10:definitiond8:s11:descriptions375:bind a registered driver to a device. A BIND GRANTS CUSTODY AND NOT I/O: it makes this driver the bound one and admits no byte. I/O ADMISSION CITES A WINDOW DECLARATION BY NAME under DEV-LAW-BIND, and where that law declares no window the act is permitted by nothing and REFUSES, recorded, citing the terminal rule — declared in DEV-LAW-BIND, never defaulted by the engine.s12:object_params6:devices12:payload_froml2:s6:devices6:drivers17:structural_paramsl2:s6:devices6:drivers4:cells1:Ss6:checksl1:d6:s4:cites10:CAP-IS-LAWs5:checks13:require_priors5:fields6:drivers5:params6:drivers6:actions19:REGISTER-CAPABILITYs7:messages53:cannot bind unregistered driver — capability is laws6:paramsd2:s6:devices8:requireds6:drivers8:requireds9:law_citeds12:DEV-LAW-BINDs4:kinds13:op_definitions4:names11:BIND-DEVICEs4:texts108:operation BIND-DEVICE: when invoked and its checks pass -> append a BIND-DEVICE decision citing DEV-LAW-BINDs4:tiers5:owners7:rule_ids14:op:BIND-DEVICEs8:polaritys1:+s9:record_ids6:rec_84d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds10:CAP-IS-LAWs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.222145+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.222193+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.222187+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi85;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions9:CREATE-OPs6:objects9:op:UNBINDs6:origind2:s12:subsystem_ids6:kernels8:built_idi0;s6:targetns7:payloadd7:s10:definitiond9:s11:descriptions553:end a device binding (remove it from the active binding view). Requires a PRIOR BIND-DEVICE naming this device: an unbind cites a bind. NOT declared `act_kind: release` — this operation takes only `device`, so any actor may end any actor's binding, which moves another actor's holdings; a release declaration would be a false sentence about what this op IS. The width of the check is stated rather than assumed: require_prior reads HISTORY, so it refuses an unbind of a never-bound device and does NOT refuse a second unbind of an already-unbound one.s12:object_params6:devices12:payload_froml1:s6:devices13:record_streams11:BIND-DEVICEs17:structural_paramsl1:s6:devices4:cells1:Ss6:checksl1:d6:s4:cites12:DEV-LAW-BINDs5:checks13:require_priors5:fields6:devices5:params6:devices6:actions11:BIND-DEVICEs7:messages76:cannot unbind a device no record ever bound — an unbind cites a prior binds6:paramsd1:s6:devices8:requireds9:law_citeds12:DEV-LAW-BINDs4:kinds13:op_definitions4:names6:UNBINDs4:texts98:operation UNBIND: when invoked and its checks pass -> append a UNBIND decision citing DEV-LAW-BINDs4:tiers5:owners7:rule_ids9:op:UNBINDs8:polaritys1:+s9:record_ids6:rec_85d16:s10:provenanced3:s10:could_readl0:s11:asserted_bys10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:sources6:systems10:rule_citeds10:CAP-IS-LAWs11:record_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.226013+00:00s12:content_forms6:inlines15:occurrence_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.226038+00:00s15:submission_times32:2026-10-02T21:53:05.226033+00:00s16:evidence_summaryns3:seqi86;s4:refsl0:s5:actors10:PC_RUNTIMEs6:actions9:CREATE-OPs6:objects15:op:AMEND-BUDGETs6: